ang theory - Black holes - Supernovas - Hubble space telescope - Quasars - Telescopes - Marie Curie - Galaxies - Periodic Table - Galileo - Newton."},"tableOfContents":"Ch. 1. Sun, sky and inspiration -- ch. 2. Christian complications -- ch. 3. Seeing the light -- ch. 4. In the beginning -- ch. 5. Relics, singularities and ripples -- ch. 6. Matter of atoms -- ch. 7. Energy to create everything -- ch. 8. Searching in the dark -- ch. 9. Exotic excursions -- ch. 10. Extraterrestrial intelligence and the baffling quasars -- ch. 11. In search of black holes -- ch. 12. Blowing it up out of all proportion -- ch. 13. Everything tied up with string -- ch. 14. Stephen Hawking's universe.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"David Filkin."},"Based on the BBC/WNET television series, in turn based on Stephen Hawking's book, A brief history of time.","Includes index."],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Hawking, Stephen",{"@type":"date","#text":"1942-2018"}]},"titleInfo":{"title":"Brief history of time"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Cosmology"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"QB981 .F48 1997"},{"@edition":"21","@authority":"ddc","#text":"523.1"}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0563383011 :"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0465081991 (Basic books)"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"gb^97067045^"},{"@type":"lccn","@invalid":"yes","#text":"^^^97033121^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"264976110"}],"relatedItem":{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990075335210203941/catalog"}},"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"QB981 .F48 1997"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Widener"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990075335210203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"007533521"}},"processingDate":"2025-05-30T05:11Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"UKM"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"971027"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20020606"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990075335210203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.6 using MARC21slim2MODS3-6.xsl\n        (Revision 1.117 2017/02/14)"}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"The  "},"title":"illustrated a brief history of time "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Hawking, Stephen,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1942-2018"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Bantam Books"},"dateIssued":"1996","edition":"Updated and expanded ed."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"248 p. : ill. ; 27 cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Color content","#text":"polychrome"}},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"The book was on the cutting edge of what was then known about the nature of the universe, but since that time there have been extraordinary advances in the technology of observing both the micro- and the macrocosmic worlds. These observations have confirmed many of Professor Hawking's theoretical predictions in the first edition of his book, including the recent discoveries of the Cosmic Background Explorer satellite (COBE), which probed back in time to within 300,000 years of the universe's beginning and revealed wrinkles in the fabric of space-time that he had projected. Eager to bring his original text the new knowledge revealed by these many observations, as well as his most recent research, for this expanded edition Professor Hawking has prepared a new introduction to the book, written an entirely new chapter on the fascinating subject of wormholes and time travel, and updated the original chapters. This edition is enhanced throughout with more than 240 full-color illustrations, including satellite images, photographs made possible by spectacular technological advances such as the Hubble Space Telescope, and computer-generated images of three and four dimensional realities."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Our picture of the universe -- Space and time -- The expanding universe -- The uncertainty principle -- Elementary particles and the forces of nature -- Black holes -- Black holes ain't so black -- The origin and fate of the universe -- The arrow of time -- Wormholes and time travel -- The unification of physics -- Conclusion -- Albert Einstein -- Galileo Galilei -- Isaac Newton -- Glossary."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Stephen Hawking."},"Originally published: A brief history of time. New York : Bantam Books, 1988.","Includes index.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes index."}],"subject":{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Cosmology"},"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"QB981 .H377 1996"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"q523.1 H392i"},{"@edition":"20","@authority":"ddc","#text":"523.1"}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0553103741"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^96019732^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"34745975"}],"relatedItem":{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990072712800203941/catalog"}},"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"QB981 .H377 1996"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Widener"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990072712800203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"007271280"}},"processingDate":"2026-09-04T04:15Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"DLC"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"960429"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20020606"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990072712800203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)"}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"A  "},"title":"brief history of time "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Hawking, Stephen,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1942-2018"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"Library of Congress Subject Headings","#text":"History."},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Informational works"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Informational works"},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Documents d'information."}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Bantam Books"},"dateIssued":"1998","edition":"Updated and expanded tenth anniversary ed."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"ix, 212 p. : ill. ; 23 cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Review","#text":"\"In the ten years since its publication in 1988, Stephen Hawking's classic work has become a landmark volume in scientific writing, with more than nine million copies in forty languages sold worldwide. That edition was on the cutting edge of what was then known about the origins and nature of the universe. But the intervening years have seen extraordinary advances in the technology of observing both the micro- and the macrocosmic worlds. These observations have confirmed many of Professor Hawking's theoretical predictions in the first edition of his book, including the recent discoveries of the Cosmic Background Explorer satellite (COBE), which probed back in time to within 300,000 years of the universe's beginning and revealed wrinkles in the fabric of space-time that he had projected.\" \"Eager to bring to his original text the new knowledge revealed by these observations, as well as his own recent research, Professor Hawking has prepared a new introduction to the book, written an entirely new chapter on wormholes and time travel, and updated the chapters throughout.\"--BOOK JACKET."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Our picture of the universe -- Space and time -- The expanding universe -- The uncertainty principle -- Elementary particles and the forces of nature -- Black holes -- Black holes ain't so black -- The origin and fate of the universe -- The arrow of time -- Wormholes and time travel -- The unification of physics -- Conclusion."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Stephen Hawking."},"Includes index.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Cosmology"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"History"}],"clas